// Stage 5 toward a Radeon that takes commands (a Polaris 10, the RX 580),
// on top of stages 0 to 4 (gpu_engines.mlx, gpu_smc_sdma.mlx): the graphics
// block's settings, its RLC, the compute processor with its KIQ and compute
// ring 0, and an indirect buffer whose fence at the end of the pipe comes
// with its interrupt. Each step is the study's
// (/tmp/brix-next/gpu-accel-kernel.verified.md 5.1 to 5.11, 4.5, 8.4 G1 to
// G4, 8.7, as its second reading corrected them), built from Linux 6.6's
// gfx_v8_0.c, with its self-test, its one line in the log and its way back.
// The rings are driven through gpu_ring.mlx, the ring as an interface.
//
// THE STEPS (../engine_choice.mlx: the digit 5):
// 5  G1  the graphics block's settings (gfx_v8_0_init_golden_registers and
//        gfx_v8_0_constants_init): the golden words where they differ, each
//        said with its old value, the SMC's clock divider word; then every
//        constant Linux sets but the display's DMIF_ADDR_CALC and the host
//        path's HDP_ADDR_CONFIG, which stage 6 writes with the picture
//        watched (the study's recommendation: the tiling words, the render
//        back ends and their raster configuration, the compute units read,
//        the shader memory words of the sixteen address spaces, the global
//        data store's, the FIFO sizes, the arbiter's order). Proved by every
//        register read back; the compute units, the shader engines and the
//        render back ends counted and said.
//    G2  the RLC: RLC_SPM_VMID (gfx_v8_0_rlc_init's one write), then
//        gfx_v8_0_rlc_resume: stopped (its serial buses waited for), reset,
//        started. Proved by its control word, the SPM word, the buses idle.
//    G3  the compute processor, the KIQ and compute ring 0 in Linux's order
//        (gfx_v8_0_cp_resume): the KIQ named to the RLC, its queue
//        descriptor made as gfx_v8_0_mqd_init makes it and its registers
//        written from it; the processor unhalted; ring 0's descriptor; the
//        doorbells' range; the KIQ's first submission SET_RESOURCES and
//        MAP_QUEUES; then the KIQ's ring test, then ring 0's. When the map
//        is not taken (ring 0's queue stays inactive) everything is put back
//        and the study's second trial runs: the same with the graphics ring
//        started between the KIQ and the map (gfx_v8_0_cp_gfx_resume and its
//        first submission, the clear state), its ring test first; the line
//        says which order worked.
//    G4  CP_ME1_PIPE0_INT_CNTL's end-of-pipe interrupt on, Linux's indirect
//        buffer test on compute ring 0 framed as Linux frames it; proved by
//        the word the buffer writes, the fence, the interrupt ring's entry
//        of source 181 for micro engine 1, pipe 0, queue 0, and the
//        interrupt.
// A step that fails puts its own registers back, last first, the
// processors halted; the steps before it stay up.
//
// THE ORDER OF A BOOT (`run`): stage 3 (F1, F2), stage 4 (D1, D2), stage 5
// (G1 to G4), each step only after the one before it passed. When the
// digit asks for 5 or more and D1 fails with copy engine 0 not reading its
// ring and no fault, Linux's order is tried (the study's D1, corrected): G1
// and G2, then D1 once more, and a line says which order worked.
//
// F2 AND STAGE 5: F2 leaves the RLC stopped and the compute processor
// halted at every boot (gpu_smc_sdma.mlx), and stage 5 begins from that
// stopped state as Linux brings the block up again from a stopped one
// (gfx_v8_0_post_soft_reset after _pre_soft_reset, hw_init after hw_fini:
// the KIQ's registers written with the compute processor halted,
// gfx_v8_0_kcq_resume's first write unhalting it). Here the copy engines'
// steps run between F2 and G1 (Linux starts the graphics block first), and
// an RLC and a compute processor running unconfigured meanwhile is a state
// Linux never stays in.
//
// THE WAY DOWN (`stop`, the study's 8.7 and 5.11): compute ring 0 unmapped
// through the KIQ while the KIQ answers, the block let go idle, the
// processors halted, a queue still active deactivated by registers, the
// RLC stopped; then stages 2 to 4's way down.
//
// THE WATCH (`heat`, the study's 7.3): once a second from the timer's tick,
// after a failed F1 (the SMC that may watch the card is held in reset) and
// while any ring runs, the card's temperature is read and said when it
// crosses a mark: at 85 C no ring takes new work, at 95 C every ring is
// stopped until the next boot. No fan or clock is touched.
//
// AROUND EVERY STEP, as stages 0 to 4 do: the temperature before and after,
// none begun at 85 C or above; the picture watched with the log's lines
// kept off the screen; the card alive afterwards. Nothing of power
// management (no DPM, clock, voltage or fan message); no register of the
// display's is written.
//
// The registers, fields and enums' values are ../registers' (generated from
// Linux 6.6's headers); the other numbers are Linux 6.6's (vi_structs.h,
// vid.h, amdgpu_doorbell.h, gfx_v8_0.c, amdgpu_gfx.c, amdgpu_ring.c,
// gmc_v8_0.c), held by tools/check_gpu_registers.py. tests/504, 505: a card.
